"""The registry-consumer smoke — spec, resolved the way a consumer resolves it.

This module exists to keep one promise: `bazel_dep(name = "spec", ...)` works
from OUTSIDE this repository. Spec's own CI builds the working tree, which is
exactly the position that could not see #58 (rules_shell dev_dependency —
invisible to spec, fatal to every consumer) or #59 (`isolate = True` rejected
by a consumer's Bazel before dev-scoping is even considered). Both shipped
green and broke the first external corpus. This module is that corpus's
position, held permanently: spec resolved FROM THE REGISTRY, its gate macros
loaded across the repo boundary, its Jena toolchains registered transitively.

⚠ The version below is the latest PUBLISHED release, deliberately NOT the
working tree — a path_override here would reintroduce the exact blindness this
module exists to remove. Bumping this pin is the last step of the release
checklist: publish to tomato-bazel/bazel-registry, then bump, and the smoke
proves the release actually resolves.

⚠ TWO POSITIONS, one module. The ROOT package loads only what the pinned
release has, and CI runs it as `//:all`. `//fanout` loads API that exists in
the working tree and not yet in any release, and CI runs it separately under
`--override_module=spec=<the tree>`. Keeping them in one module means the
unreleased-API check is a REAL consumer's check — same registries, same
resolution, same repo boundary — rather than a build inside spec wearing a
consumer's name.

The dep block mirrors the first real consumer verbatim. rules_rdf is loaded
directly (rdf_dataset), so it needs a direct dep; rules_jena registers all four
toolchains itself, non-dev — it is pinned here only so the Jena toolchain
definitions resolve against the rules_rdf version they were written for
(bzlmod is highest-version-wins, and a silent skew is the failure mode).
"""

module(
    name = "spec_smoke_consumer",
    version = "0.0.0",
)

bazel_dep(name = "spec", version = "0.7.0")

bazel_dep(name = "rules_rdf", version = "0.3.0")

# For this module's own py_test, NOT for spec_workorders — that macro carries
# its assembler and toolchain inside @spec, which //:workorders proves by
# building without this line. A consumer writing its own python tests declares
# rules_python the way any module declares what it loads by name.
bazel_dep(name = "rules_python", version = "1.7.0")
bazel_dep(name = "rules_jena", version = "0.3.2")
